f 1071 "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2.$ Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078231 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =0+2(x+2) \\\\  & =2(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2(0.2) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078232 #edb/ref 1069 "e-292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078232 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4,$ estimate the value of $g(3.6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078232 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =g(a)+{g}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =g(4)+{g}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3,$ and since $g'(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ then $g'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3.6) & ≈L(3.6) \\\\  & =3+2(3.6-4) \\\\  & =3+2(-0.4) \\\\  & =3-0.8 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078236 #edb/ref 1069 "e-293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078236 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0).$ If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078236 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5),$ so we have $f(-2)=-5.$ Also, we're told that $f'(-2)=3,$ so we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5+3(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.3) & ≈L(-2.3) \\\\  & =-5+3(-2.3+2) \\\\  & =-5+3(-0.3) \\\\  & =-5-0.9 \\\\  & =-5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078566 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078567 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "580243c0-444d-51d9-820f-ab39d2e434fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078972 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = 5x - 3$ and $f(2) =-1$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ to estimate the value of $f(2.4)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70b30b18-9330-5710-9320-15027fb2107e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078972 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078997 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(-2, 2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-3,-1)$. If $f^{′}(-2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x =-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab2f6e79-7a14-5396-9023-6cf5ec22b244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078997 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1061 4825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80d363a5-ade6-554f-b554-78362a2a4a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1061 4826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29daedd8-9104-56cf-8960-bc6517ab9536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1061 4829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f65d689-6eed-51f9-99e7-de9d4bff5796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1061 4832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "539a8b72-5dd7-5bd3-b6ff-ecd4fb4535ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1061 4833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{'}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & \\approx L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1065 579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1065 582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1067 "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295350 #edb/ref 1080 "11.8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295350 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295350 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e3d35ca-ccf6-5b18-8a5e-78467bdfc92c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296917 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296917 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296917 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186295350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296917 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8a43d106-9839-5bde-9a42-b20c59c9fdb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1071 "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce7b136c-2ab3-5bfd-98fd-46fda48c1806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297412 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317787 #edb/ref 1080 "2.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4466b5c4-7137-58ba-bc9b-618a5d1ada38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318911 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318911 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318911 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318911 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318911 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e78dd783-f2e3-59e1-b7f8-93cab4470141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(-4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(-4)=6.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-4,$ estimate the value of $f(-3.8).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-3.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e03e29d0-50e9-528b-9c71-b52ed36384c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319052 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-4)+{f}^{′}(-4)(x-(-4)) \\\\  & =1+6(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-3.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.8) & ≈L(-3.8) \\\\  & =1+6(-3.8+4) \\\\  & =1+6(0.2) \\\\  & =1+1.2 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327144 #edb/ref 1080 "8.75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "185bd225-27ea-5674-95ca-a56c74418c0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327567 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327567 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327567 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48bd7b8a-015d-50ab-bb38-609edbfcaab3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328052 #edb/ref 1080 "9.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328052 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328052 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81aeeb53-2a67-5879-907e-d250a9dcc6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328145 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328145 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328145 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ccede01-5805-5b15-954a-9a60f2ec5889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328651 #edb/ref 1080 "8.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328651 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328651 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3ef5572-1d9c-55ef-acf9-cb700da795f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186327567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329471 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a6a72543-d7b3-5d16-9023-ad6d4845370a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ and $f(2)=4.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4ab790a-069c-5f5b-8f9d-fdcc980a31a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329871 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=4,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2x,$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=4+4(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =4+4(3-2) \\\\  & =4+4(1) \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(3,-3)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ estimate the value of $f(3.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23fa4cab-6951-5a21-810d-7733098a8e5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402957 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(3,-3),$ so we have $f(3)=-3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-3+4(x-3) \\\\  & =-3+4x-12 \\\\  & =4x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈L(3.2) \\\\  & =4(3.2)-15 \\\\  & =12.8-15 \\\\  & =-2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402958 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7c924a1-67fd-5e0c-8035-7de5293c5ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402959 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an overestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5cf36fa0-f53d-5a7d-b768-a8a6d96fa3d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402960 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an overestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402960 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402960 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c863ec26-7875-52c6-8e8e-ef3828506796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402961 #edb/ref 1080 "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an underestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402961